---
import Doc from '../../layouts/Doc.astro';
import Callout from '../../components/Callout.astro';
import MessageDecoder from '../../components/MessageDecoder.astro';
---

<Doc
  title="Messages"
  slug="messages"
  description="MIDI 1.0 message reference: status and data bytes, channel voice and mode messages, system common and real-time messages, and running status, with byte-level tables."
  specs={[
    { label: 'MIDI 1.0 Core Specifications', href: 'https://midi.org/midi-1-0-core-specifications' },
    { label: 'All specs', href: 'https://midi.org/specs' },
  ]}
>
  <p class="lede">
    A MIDI 1.0 stream is a sequence of 8-bit bytes. Each message is one <em>status byte</em> followed by zero, one, or
    two <em>data bytes</em>. System Exclusive is the one exception: it can carry any number of data bytes.
  </p>

  <MessageDecoder initial="90 3C 64" level={2} />

  <h2 id="status-and-data">Status bytes and data bytes</h2>
  <p>
    The most significant bit tells the two kinds of byte apart. Status bytes have the top bit set (<code>0x80</code>–<code
      >0xFF</code
    >). Data bytes have it clear (<code>0x00</code>–<code>0x7F</code>), so every data value is 7 bits wide: 0–127.
  </p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Byte</th><th>Bits</th><th>Range</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><span class="b b-s">status</span></td>
          <td><code>1sss nnnn</code></td>
          <td><code>0x80</code>–<code>0xFF</code></td>
          <td>
            For channel messages, the high nibble <code>sss</code> is the message type and the low nibble <code>nnnn</code> is the
            channel.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><span class="b b-d">data</span></td>
          <td><code>0vvv vvvv</code></td>
          <td><code>0x00</code>–<code>0x7F</code></td>
          <td>Parameter values such as note number, velocity, controller number, or controller value.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    Because the two ranges never overlap, a receiver can always resynchronize: whenever it sees a byte with the high bit
    set, a new message starts.
  </p>

  <h3 id="channels">Channels</h3>
  <p>
    Channel messages carry the channel in the low nibble of the status byte. People count channels 1–16, but the nibble
    holds 0–15. <span class="b b-s">90</span> is Note On on channel 1 and <span class="b b-s">9F</span> is Note On on
    channel 16. In tables on this site, <code>n</code> stands for that channel nibble.
  </p>

  <h2 id="channel-voice">Channel voice messages</h2>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Status</th><th>Message</th><th>Data 1</th><th>Data 2</th><th class="num">Length</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><span class="b b-s">8n</span></td>
          <td>Note Off</td>
          <td><span class="b b-d">kk</span> note number</td>
          <td><span class="b b-d">vv</span> release velocity</td>
          <td class="num">3</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><span class="b b-s">9n</span></td>
          <td>Note On</td>
          <td><span class="b b-d">kk</span> note number</td>
          <td><span class="b b-d">vv</span> velocity (0 = Note Off)</td>
          <td class="num">3</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><span class="b b-s">An</span></td>
          <td>Polyphonic Key Pressure (poly aftertouch)</td>
          <td><span class="b b-d">kk</span> note number</td>
          <td><span class="b b-d">vv</span> pressure</td>
          <td class="num">3</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><span class="b b-s">Bn</span></td>
          <td>Control Change</td>
          <td><span class="b b-d">cc</span> controller number (0–119)</td>
          <td><span class="b b-d">vv</span> value</td>
          <td class="num">3</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><span class="b b-s">Cn</span></td>
          <td>Program Change</td>
          <td><span class="b b-d">pp</span> program number</td>
          <td>none</td>
          <td class="num">2</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><span class="b b-s">Dn</span></td>
          <td>Channel Pressure (channel aftertouch)</td>
          <td><span class="b b-d">vv</span> pressure</td>
          <td>none</td>
          <td class="num">2</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><span class="b b-s">En</span></td>
          <td>Pitch Bend Change</td>
          <td><span class="b b-d">ll</span> LSB (bits 0–6)</td>
          <td><span class="b b-d">mm</span> MSB (bits 7–13)</td>
          <td class="num">3</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h3 id="note-on-velocity-zero">Note On with velocity 0</h3>
  <p>
    A Note On with velocity 0 means exactly the same as a Note Off with velocity 64. Many devices send
    <span class="b b-s">9n</span>&nbsp;<span class="b b-d">kk</span>&nbsp;<span class="b b-d">00</span> in place of a
    real Note Off so they can stay in running status (see below). Receivers <strong>must</strong> treat both forms as
    note-off.
  </p>

  <h3 id="pitch-bend">Pitch bend</h3>
  <p>
    Pitch bend is a 14-bit value, sent LSB first: <code>value = (MSB &lt;&lt; 7) | LSB</code>. It runs from 0 to 16383,
    and 8192 (<code>0x2000</code>, sent as <span class="b b-d">00</span>&nbsp;<span class="b b-d">40</span>) means no
    bend. How far the extremes bend is set by the receiver, normally ±2 semitones. The <a href="/docs/rpn-nrpn/">pitch bend
    sensitivity RPN</a> changes that range. See <a href="/docs/notes/#pitch-bend">Notes &amp; Pitch</a> for the math.
  </p>

  <h3 id="program-change">Program change numbering</h3>
  <p>
    Program Change data runs from 0 to 127 on the wire. Synth front panels and the General MIDI list usually show it as
    1–128. To reach more than 128 sounds, send a <a href="/docs/control-change/">Bank Select</a> (CC 0 and CC 32) first,
    then the Program Change.
  </p>

  <h2 id="channel-mode">Channel mode messages</h2>
  <p>
    Controller numbers 120–127 do not control sound parameters. They are reserved for <em>channel mode messages</em>, sent
    as <span class="b b-s">Bn</span>&nbsp;<span class="b b-d">78</span>–<span class="b b-d">7F</span>: All Sound Off,
    Reset All Controllers, Local Control, All Notes Off, and the Omni and Mono/Poly mode switches. The full list is on the
    <a href="/docs/control-change/#channel-mode">Control Change</a> page.
  </p>

  <h2 id="system-common">System common messages</h2>
  <p>System messages have no channel. They apply to the whole device or the whole MIDI cable.</p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Status</th><th>Message</th><th>Data</th><th class="num">Length</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><span class="b b-m">F0</span></td>
          <td>System Exclusive start</td>
          <td>Manufacturer ID, then any number of data bytes, ended by <span class="b b-m">F7</span></td>
          <td class="num">var.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><span class="b b-s">F1</span></td>
          <td>MIDI Time Code Quarter Frame</td>
          <td><span class="b b-d">0nnn dddd</span>: piece number <code>nnn</code>, value nibble <code>dddd</code></td>
          <td class="num">2</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><span class="b b-s">F2</span></td>
          <td>Song Position Pointer</td>
          <td><span class="b b-d">ll</span> <span class="b b-d">mm</span>: 14-bit count of MIDI beats (1 beat = 6 clocks = a 16th note)</td>
          <td class="num">3</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><span class="b b-s">F3</span></td>
          <td>Song Select</td>
          <td><span class="b b-d">ss</span> song number 0–127</td>
          <td class="num">2</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><span class="b b-s">F4</span></td>
          <td>Undefined (reserved)</td>
          <td>none</td>
          <td class="num">n/a</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><span class="b b-s">F5</span></td>
          <td>Undefined (reserved)</td>
          <td>none. Some vendors use it unofficially as a port/cable select.</td>
          <td class="num">n/a</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><span class="b b-s">F6</span></td>
          <td>Tune Request</td>
          <td>none. Asks analog synths to retune their oscillators.</td>
          <td class="num">1</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><span class="b b-m">F7</span></td>
          <td>End of Exclusive (EOX)</td>
          <td>none. Ends a SysEx message.</td>
          <td class="num">1</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    SysEx has its own page: <a href="/docs/sysex/">System Exclusive</a>. Time code and song position are covered in
    <a href="/docs/timing/">Timing &amp; Sync</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="system-real-time">System real-time messages</h2>
  <p>
    Real-time messages are single status bytes with no data. They carry timing, so they have top priority. They may be
    sent <strong>at any point in the stream</strong>, even between the bytes of another message and inside a SysEx dump.
    A receiver should act on a real-time byte immediately and then carry on parsing the surrounding message as though the
    real-time byte had not been there.
  </p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Status</th><th>Message</th><th>Notes</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><span class="b b-s">F8</span></td><td>Timing Clock</td><td>Sent 24 times per quarter note while the clock is running.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><span class="b b-s">F9</span></td><td>Undefined (reserved)</td><td>Once proposed as a 10 ms "Tick". Not part of the standard.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><span class="b b-s">FA</span></td><td>Start</td><td>Start playback from the beginning of the song (position 0).</td></tr>
        <tr><td><span class="b b-s">FB</span></td><td>Continue</td><td>Resume from the current song position.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><span class="b b-s">FC</span></td><td>Stop</td><td>Stop playback and keep the current position.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><span class="b b-s">FD</span></td><td>Undefined (reserved)</td><td>none</td></tr>
        <tr>
          <td><span class="b b-s">FE</span></td>
          <td>Active Sensing</td>
          <td>
            Optional keep-alive. A sender that uses it transmits at least every 300 ms. When a receiver has seen it once and
            then hears nothing for more than 300 ms, it should turn off all voices and go back to normal operation.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><span class="b b-s">FF</span></td>
          <td>System Reset</td>
          <td>Reset the receiver to its power-up state. Use it sparingly and never send it automatically at power-up, or two devices can reset each other in a loop.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2 id="running-status">Running status</h2>
  <p>
    If a channel message has the same status byte as the message before it, the sender may leave the status byte out and
    send only the data bytes. The receiver remembers the last channel status (the <em>running status</em>) and applies it
    to any data bytes that arrive without one. On a 5-pin DIN cable this cuts note traffic by up to a third.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Only <strong>channel</strong> messages (<code>0x80</code>–<code>0xEF</code>) set the running status.</li>
    <li>
      <strong>System common</strong> messages and SysEx (<code>0xF0</code>–<code>0xF7</code>) <strong>clear</strong> it.
      The sender must send the next channel message with its status byte.
    </li>
    <li><strong>Real-time</strong> messages (<code>0xF8</code>–<code>0xFF</code>) do not affect running status.</li>
    <li>Data bytes that arrive while no running status is set should be ignored.</li>
  </ul>
  <Callout title="Why devices send Note On, velocity 0">
    <p>
      With running status, a stream of Note Ons and Note Offs keeps switching between <span class="b b-s">9n</span> and
      <span class="b b-s">8n</span>, and every switch costs a status byte. Sending note-offs as Note On with velocity 0
      means a long run of notes needs only one status byte.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h3 id="worked-example">Worked example</h3>
  <p>
    The bytes below play a C major triad on channel 1 and then release it. A MIDI clock byte lands in the middle of a
    message:
  </p>
  <pre><code>90 3C 64 40 64 43 64   F8   3C 00 40 F8 00 43 00</code></pre>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Bytes</th><th>Decoded</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><span class="b b-s">90</span> <span class="b b-d">3C</span> <span class="b b-d">64</span></td>
          <td>Note On, ch 1, C4 (60), velocity 100. Running status is now <code>0x90</code>.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr><td><span class="b b-d">40</span> <span class="b b-d">64</span></td><td>Running status: Note On, E4 (64), velocity 100.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><span class="b b-d">43</span> <span class="b b-d">64</span></td><td>Running status: Note On, G4 (67), velocity 100.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><span class="b b-s">F8</span></td><td>Timing Clock. Running status is unchanged.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><span class="b b-d">3C</span> <span class="b b-d">00</span></td><td>Note On, C4, velocity 0, which means Note Off.</td></tr>
        <tr>
          <td><span class="b b-d">40</span> <span class="b b-s">F8</span> <span class="b b-d">00</span></td>
          <td>A Timing Clock arrives inside the message. Handle it, then finish: Note Off for E4.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr><td><span class="b b-d">43</span> <span class="b b-d">00</span></td><td>Note Off for G4.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2 id="wire-timing">Timing on the wire</h2>
  <p>
    Classic serial MIDI runs at <strong>31,250 bits per second</strong>, 8-N-1: one start bit, eight data bits, no
    parity, one stop bit. That is 10 bits per byte, so each byte takes <strong>320&nbsp;µs</strong>, a 3-byte message
    takes about <strong>0.96&nbsp;ms</strong>, and a cable carries at most about 3,125 bytes per second. A ten-note chord
    therefore takes around 6–10 ms to send in full, which is one reason running status matters. USB and network
    transports send messages in packets at much higher rates; see <a href="/docs/transports/">Transports</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="parsing-checklist">Parser checklist</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>On a byte <code>&gt;= 0xF8</code>: handle the real-time message right away and change nothing else.</li>
    <li>On <code>0xF0</code>: start collecting SysEx and clear running status.</li>
    <li>
      On <code>0xF7</code>, or on any other status byte while collecting SysEx: end the SysEx. A non-EOX status byte also
      ends a SysEx, after which you process it normally.
    </li>
    <li>On <code>0xF1</code>–<code>0xF6</code>: clear running status and read the message's fixed number of data bytes.</li>
    <li>On <code>0x80</code>–<code>0xEF</code>: set running status and expect 1 or 2 data bytes.</li>
    <li>On a data byte: add it to the current message. Once the message is complete, emit it and stay in running status.</li>
  </ol>
</Doc>
